","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σn_products * S_products - Σn_reactants * S_subproducts

where n_products and n_reactants are the number of moles of products and reactants, respectively, and S_products and S_subproducts are the standard molar entropies of the products and reactants.

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + 3 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

The standard molar entropies (S) at 298 K are:

S(CO) = 198.6 J/mol·K
S(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
S(CO2) = 213.6 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = (3 mol * 213.6 J/mol·K) - (3 mol * 198.6 J/mol·K) - (3 mol * 205.1 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 681.8 J/K - 595.8 J/K - 615.1 J/K

ΔS = -654.1 J/K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -654.1 J/K.
