Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the entropy change (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

\[
\Delta S = \sum \nu_p S_p - \sum \nu_r S_r
\]

where:
- \(\nu_p\) and \(\nu_r\) are the stoichiometric coefficients of the products and reactants, respectively.
- \(S_p\) and \(S_r\) are the molar entropies of the products and reactants, respectively.

For the given reaction:

\[
2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g)
\]

The entropy change is:

\[
\Delta S = [2 \times 66.5 + 2 \times 248.2] - [2 \times 91.2 + 3 \times 205.1]
\]

\[
\Delta S = [133.0 + 496.4] - [182.4 + 615.3]
\]

\[
\Delta S = 609.4 - 797.7
\]

\[
\Delta S = -398.5 \, \text{J/mol K}
\]

Thus, the entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-398.5 \, \text{J/mol K}}\).
