"Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is:  
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative:  
\[
f'(a) = \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}
\]  
This represents the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \).  

The equation \( y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \) is in the form \( y = mx + b \), where \( m = f'(a) \) (the slope) and \( b = f(a) - f'(a)a \) (the y-intercept).  

Thus, the equation is derived from the definition of the derivative and expresses the tangent line's slope and location.
