A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Convert the rates to meters per second**:  
   - Jogger speed: \( 9 \, \text{km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \, \text{m}}{1 \, \text{km}} \times \frac{1 \, \text{hr}}{60 \, \text{hr}} = 2.5 \, \text{m/s} \).  
   - Train speed: \( 45 \, \text{km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \, \text{m}}{1 \, \text{km}} \times \frac{1 \, \text{hr}}{60 \, \text{hr}} = 10 \, \text{m/s} \).  

2. **Determine the relative speed**:  
   - The train is faster than the jogger, so the relative speed is \( 10 \, \text{m/s} - 2.5 \, \text{m/s} = 7.5 \, \text{m/s} \).  

3. **Calculate the total distance covered by the train**:  
   - The time taken by the train to pass the jogger is 25 seconds.  
   - The total distance covered by the train is \( 9.5 \, \text{m/s} \times 25 \, \text{s} = 375 \, \text{m} \).  

4. **Determine the length of the train**:  
   - The total distance covered by the train is the sum of the jogger's distance (150 m) and the length of the train.  
   - The length of the train is \( 375 \, \text{m} - 150 \, \text{m} = 250 \, \text{m} \).  

   Therefore, the length of the train is \(\boxed{250 \, \text{m}}\).
