</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
First, let's convert the speeds of the jogger and the train from km/hr to m/s, because the time given is in seconds and the distance we want to find is in meters.

Speed in m/s = Speed in km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s)

Jogger's speed in m/s = 9 km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s)
Jogger's speed in m/s = 9 * 1000 / 3600
Jogger's speed in m/s = 2.5 m/s

Train's speed in m/s = 45 km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s)
Train's speed in m/s = 45 * 1000 / 3600
Train's speed in m/s = 12.5 m/s

Relative speed of the train with respect to the jogger is the difference between their speeds because they are moving in the same direction.

Relative speed in m/s = Train's speed in m/s - Jogger's speed in m/s
Relative speed in m/s = 12.5 m/s - 2.5 m/s
Relative speed in m/s = 10 m/s

The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. The distance the train covers in this time is the length of the train plus the initial 150 m head start the jogger had.

Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = Relative speed in m/s * Time in seconds
Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = 10 m/s * 25 s
Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = 250 m

Since the jogger was initially 150 m ahead, we subtract this distance from the total distance covered by the train to find the length of the train.

Length of the train = Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds - Initial distance ahead of the jogger
Length of the train = 250 m - 150 m
Length of the train = 100 m

Therefore, the length of the train is $\boxed{100}$  meters.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

