<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length \(l_0\) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{\lambda} \left( \frac{n_{\text{eff}}^2 - n_f^2}{n_f^2} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
where:  
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the wave.  
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the cladding.  
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the wave.  

Given:  
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\)  
- \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{m}\)  

First, we need to find \(n_f\). The refractive index of Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As is approximately 1.65 at 1 μm.  

Substituting the values:  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \left( \frac{1.65^2 - (1.65 - 0.3)^2}{1.65^2} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \left( \frac{1.65^2 - 1.35^2}{1.65^2} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \left( \frac{2.7425 - 0.94025}{2.7425} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \left( \frac{1.60225}{2.7425} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \left( 0.576 \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
l_0 = \frac{1}{1 \times 10^{-7}} \times 0.741
\]  
\[
l_0 = 7.41 \times 10^5 \, \text{m}
\]  

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{7.41 \times 10^5 \, \text{m}}\).
