A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To solve this, we use the time dilation formula:  
\[ t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}} \]  
where:  
- \( t \) is the time recorded by the stationary body (Earth)  
- \( t_0 \) is the time recorded by the moving body (the cockpit clock)  
- \( v \) is the velocity of the moving body (0.6c)  
- \( c \) is the speed of light  

1. **Given:**  
   - \( t_0 = 3 \text{ years} \)  
   - \( v = 0.6c \)  

2. **Substitute into the formula:**  
   \[ t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{0.6c}{c}\right)^2}} \]  
   \[ t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} \]  
   \[ t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} \]  
   \[ t = \frac{3}{0.8} \]  
   \[ t = 3.75 \text{ years} \]  

3. **Final Answer:**  
   The time shown on the cartdoor bay clock (relative to Earth) is \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\).
