The Dufort–Frankel method for the one-dimensional diffusion equation \(\frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} = \kappa \frac{\partial^2 \phi}{\partial x^2}\) is given by:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Show that this method produces stable, non-growing solutions for any \(\Delta t\).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To show that the Dufort–Frankel method is stable for any \(\Delta t\), we analyze the stability of the equation by considering the time-dependent solution \(\phi_j^{n}\) as a series of terms:
\[
\phi_j^{n} = \phi_0^{n} + \varepsilon_j^{n},
\]
where \(\phi_0^{n}\) is the steady-state solution (a constant) and \(\varepsilon_j^{n}\) is a small error term.

Substituting this into the Dufort–Frankel equation:
\[
\phi_0^{n+1} + \varepsilon_j^{n+1} = \phi_0^{n-1} + \varepsilon_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^{n} - \phi_{j+1}^{n+1} - \phi_{j-1}^{n} + \phi_{j-1}^{n+1} \right).
\]

Since \(\phi_0^{n}\) is a constant, it cancels out from both sides. The equation simplifies to:
\[
\varepsilon_{j}^{n+1} = \varepsilon_{j}^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^{n} - \phi_{j+1}^{n+1} - \phi_{j-1}^{n} + \phi_{j-1}^{n+1} \right).
\]

