</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The contribution to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) due to hard particles in a metal is given by the Kohard-Oensley equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{G b}{d} \left( \frac{1}{2} \frac{d}{d_p} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
where:  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(d\) is the average center-to-center spacing of the particles,  
- \(d_p\) is the diameter of the particles.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \, \text{N m}^{-2} \times 0.286 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m}}{6 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m}} \left( \frac{1}{2} \frac{6 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m}}{10^{-8} \, \text{m}} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 1.05 \times 10^6 \, \text{N m}^{-2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 1.05 \, \text{MPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength of the alloy is \(\boxed{1.05 \, \text{MPa}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

