Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), \(V_3 = 0\). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_1 & V_2 & 0 \end{vmatrix}
\]
Expanding the determinant, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_2 & 0 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} - \begin{{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_1 & 0 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{j} + \begin{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \\ V_1 & V_2 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{k}
