","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2h}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(h\) is Planck's constant (\(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),  
- \(m_e\) is the mass of an electron (\(9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the angle of scattering.  

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the electron is back-scattered). Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2h}{m_e c} (1 - \cos 180^\circ) = \frac{2h}{m_e c} \times 2 = \frac{4h}{m_e c}
\]  

The maximum percentage change is:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]  
where \(\lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\).  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{4h}{m_e c} \right) \times \left( \frac{1}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]  
\[
= \left( \frac{4 \times 6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31} \times 3 \times 10^8} \right) \times \left( \frac{1}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100
\]  
\[
