A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Coulomb's law:  
\[
F = qE = ma
\]  
Solving for \(a\):  
\[
a = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.60 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.125 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.125 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The time taken by the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
Since the proton starts from rest (\(u = 0\)), the time is:  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{3.125 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 1.11 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{s}
\]  
Thus, the time taken is \(\boxed{1.11 \, \text{ms}}\).
