","The covariant derivative of a vector \( V^\mu \) in closed spacetime is given by:
\[
\nabla_\alpha V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\alpha} + \frac{\partial V^\mu}{dx^\alpha} \begin{pmatrix} \begin{array}{c} \cdots \\ \text{d}x_i \end{array} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \begin{array}{c} \cdots \\ \text{d}x_j \end{array} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \begin{array}{c} \cdots \\ \text{d}x_j \end{array} \end{pmatrix}
\]
where \( \nabla_\alpha \) is the coariant derivative with respect to \( x^\alpha \), \( V^\mu \) is the vector, and \( \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\alpha} \) is the partial derivative of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\alpha \). The second term is the gradient of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\alpha \), and it accounts for the non-linearity of the coordinate space.

The covariant derivative is defined as the partial derivative plus the gradient of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\alpha \). This ensures that the covariant derivative is a vector that is independent of the choice of coordinate space. This is important for the fact that the covariant derivative is used to define the gradient of the field \( V^\mu \) in the context of the special action.
