","The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system is given by the following vector:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_1 & V_2 & V_3 \end{vmatrix}
\]
where \(\mathbf{V} = V_1 \mathbf{i} + V_2 \mathbf{j} + V_3 \mathbf{k}\).

Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), \(V_3 = 0\). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_1 & V_2 & 0 \end{vmatrix}
\]
Expanding the determinant, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_2 & 0 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} - \begin{{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_1 & 0 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{j} + \begin{vmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \\ V_1 & V_2 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(0 - 0\right) \mathbf{i} - \left(0 - 0\right) \mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y}\right) \mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y}\right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Thus, the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y}\right) \mathbf{k}
\]
