\[
w = \frac{P_1 V_1}{\gamma - 1} \left[ \left( \frac{P_2}{P_1} \right)^{1/(\gamma - 1)} - 1 \right],
\]

where:
- \( P_1 = 1.0 \times 10^3 \) bar (initial pressure),
- \( V_1 = 1.0 \) L (initial volume),
- \( P_2 = 1.0 \) bar (ambient pressure),
- \( \gamma = 1.4 \).

Substituting these values and accounting for the 70% energy in the pressure wave:

\[
w = (0.70) \left( \frac{1.0 \times 10^3 \text{ bar} \times 1.0 \text{ L}}{1.4 - 1} \right) \left[ \left( \frac{1.0}{1.0 \times 10^3} \right)^{1/0.4} - 1 \right].
\]

Simplifying the exponent:

\[
\left( \frac{1.0}{1.0 \times 10^3} \right)^{2.5} = (10^{-3})^{2.5} = 10^{-7.5} \approx 0.
\]

Thus:

\[
w = (0.70) \left( \frac{1.0 \times 10^3 \text{ bar} \cdot \text{L}}{0.4} \right) (-1) = -1.75 \times 10^3 \text{ bar} \cdot \text{L}.
\]

